<p>Customers who use Airtel Money can pay at the till the same way M-Pesa customers do: a prompt on their phone, or a transaction ID the
  cashier types in. You need an Airtel Money merchant account and API access from Airtel Africa.</p>

<h2>Connecting your merchant account</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Register on Airtel Africa's developer portal and create an app for collections in Kenya. Airtel gives you a <strong>client ID</strong>
    and a <strong>client secret</strong>: first for the sandbox (test money), then for production once Airtel approves you.</li>
  <li>In HardPoint, open <strong>Settings › Airtel Money</strong> and press <strong>Add Airtel Money</strong>.</li>
  <li>Give it a name, choose <strong>Sandbox</strong> or <strong>Production</strong>, and type your merchant or till number if you want it
    shown to cashiers. Paste the client ID and secret. They're stored encrypted and never shown again.</li>
  <li>Choose the branch it's for, or leave it for <strong>every branch without its own</strong>.</li>
  <li>Press <strong>Test connection</strong>. If it says it's connected, the keys are right.</li>
  <li>Copy the <strong>callback address</strong> shown on the page into your app on Airtel's developer portal. That's how Airtel tells
    HardPoint a customer has paid. Keep it private: it identifies your shop.</li>
</ol>
<p>Only the shop's owner can connect Airtel Money. To stop taking it, edit the account and untick <strong>In use</strong>.</p>

<h2>Taking a payment with a prompt</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Ring up the sale and choose <strong>Airtel</strong> among the payment methods. It's there once Airtel Money is connected for the branch.</li>
  <li>Type the customer's Airtel number (for example 0733 000 111) and press <strong>Send prompt</strong>. The amount is rounded up to the
    next whole shilling.</li>
  <li>The customer gets a message on their phone and enters their Airtel Money PIN. The till waits and completes the sale by itself when
    Airtel confirms, then prints the receipt with the Airtel Money transaction ID.</li>
  <li>If the customer declines, has too little money or doesn't answer within a few minutes, the till says so. Try again, or take
    another payment method.</li>
</ol>
<p>Press <strong>Stop waiting</strong> if the customer walks away. If they pay anyway, the money isn't lost: see below.</p>

<h2>Typing a transaction ID instead</h2>
<p>If the customer has already paid your merchant number from their phone, choose <strong>Airtel</strong>, type the transaction ID
  from their confirmation message in the <strong>Code</strong> box and press <strong>Take payment</strong>. The ID is required, so every Airtel payment can be
  traced. This also works on the offline till.</p>

<h2>Money that arrived after the till stopped waiting</h2>
<p>When a prompt is paid after the cashier stopped waiting, the payment shows on the till under <strong>Received on Airtel Money, not used
  yet</strong>, with the time, phone number and amount. Press <strong>Use</strong> next to it to put it on the current sale. Each payment
  can be used once.</p>

<h2>Refunds, deposits and accounts</h2>
<p>Airtel Money is a payment method for order deposits, customers paying their accounts, paying suppliers and refunds on returns. A refund
  recorded as Airtel Money is a note of how you paid the customer back; send the money from your Airtel merchant account yourself.</p>

<h2>Reports and tax</h2>
<p>Airtel Money has its own line in <strong>Payments by method</strong>, on the X and Z reports and in the daily summary email. KRA eTIMS
  receives it as a mobile money payment, and the QuickBooks export posts it to its own account.</p>

<h2>If something goes wrong</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>"Couldn't connect"</strong> when testing: check you chose the environment (sandbox or production) that matches the keys.</li>
  <li><strong>Prompts never arrive:</strong> check the number is an Airtel line and the customer's phone has signal.</li>
  <li><strong>The till keeps waiting:</strong> HardPoint also asks Airtel directly after about 20 seconds, so a lost callback doesn't stop
    the sale. Check the callback address on Airtel's portal matches the one in Settings.</li>
</ul>
